CAT = "nzlcqOffences"; SEG = "OFF"
Q = [
("In New Zealand, a minor for the purposes of alcohol law is a person under:",
 "18", ["20", "21", "16"],
 "A minor is a person under 18. The purchase age is 18 for both on-licences and off-licences."),
("Which of these is an approved evidence of age document in New Zealand?",
 "A current Kiwi Access card", ["A current student ID card", "A bank card with a photo", "A work security pass"],
 "Approved evidence of age documents are a current passport, a current New Zealand driver licence and a current Kiwi Access card (formerly the HANZ 18+ card), and since 28 May 2026 an approved digital ID credential."),
("A tourist shows a current driver licence from another country. Is it an approved evidence of age document?",
 "No, only NZ licences count", ["Yes, any driver licence", "Yes, if it is in English", "Only for wine and beer"],
 "Overseas driver licences are not approved evidence of age. A visitor can use a current passport, which is approved whatever country issued it."),
("A visitor from Germany wants a beer and shows a current German passport. What is correct?",
 "It is approved evidence of age", ["Only a NZ passport counts", "A copy must be kept on file", "It must be shown with a licence"],
 "A current passport from any country is an approved evidence of age document."),
("A patron's New Zealand driver licence passed its expiry date last year. Is it approved evidence of age?",
 "No, it must be current", ["Yes, if the photo matches", "Yes, with a second card", "Yes, on weekdays only"],
 "Approved documents must be current. An expired licence is not approved evidence of age."),
("A young-looking patron shows a photo of his passport on his phone. What should staff do?",
 "Ask for approved ID instead", ["Accept the phone photo", "Serve a low-alcohol drink", "Ask his friends to confirm his age"],
 "Photos, photocopies and scans are not approved evidence of age. Staff need a current original document, or an approved digital ID credential from an accredited service (allowed since 28 May 2026)."),
("A 17-year-old orders a beer, and his 19-year-old friend says he will vouch for him. What should staff do?",
 "Refuse to serve the 17-year-old", ["Serve him a single drink", "Serve him if the friend pays for it", "Serve a low-alcohol beer"],
 "Selling or supplying alcohol to a minor on licensed premises is an offence, whoever vouches for them."),
("A 19-year-old buys a jug of beer and pours glasses for her 16-year-old cousin. What has happened?",
 "Alcohol supplied to a minor", ["Nothing, she is an adult", "A lawful family supply", "A breach only if it is wine"],
 "On licensed premises a minor must not be supplied with alcohol by others; staff must watch for adults buying for minors."),
("Outside licensed premises, who may supply alcohol to a 16-year-old without needing anyone else's consent?",
 "Their parent or legal guardian", ["Any adult family friend", "Any older brother or sister", "Anyone over 25"],
 "Off licensed premises, only a parent or legal guardian may supply, or someone with the parent's or guardian's express consent, and the supply must be responsible."),
("A friend's parent sends a text saying their 17-year-old may have two beers at your barbecue. Under the Act, the text can be:",
 "Express consent", ["Never enough consent", "Valid only on paper", "Valid only if notarised"],
 "Express consent can be given by a conversation, email or text the supplier has good reason to believe is genuine. The supply must still be responsible."),
("Which is an offence for a licensee or manager under the Act?",
 "Allowing a patron to get drunk", ["Serving low-alcohol beer", "Closing earlier than the LAP allows", "Offering free water"],
 "It is an offence to allow a person to become intoxicated on licensed premises, and to sell or supply alcohol to an intoxicated person."),
("An intoxicated patron is sitting quietly with a glass of water. May the venue let him stay?",
 "No, he must leave the premises", ["Yes, if he has stopped drinking", "Yes, until closing time", "Yes, if a friend watches him"],
 "Licensees and managers must not allow an intoxicated person to remain on licensed premises; he must be asked to leave and helped to get home safely."),
("A bar advertises \"Drink till you drop: unlimited drinks for one price\". Why is this unlawful?",
 "It encourages excessive drinking", ["Fixed prices are banned", "Only clubs may run specials", "Specials must be approved by police"],
 "Promoting or advertising alcohol in a way that encourages people to drink excessively is an offence."),
("A poster outside a bottle store announces 30% off all wine. Why is this a problem?",
 "25%+ off is visible outside", ["Wine may never be discounted", "Only supermarkets may discount", "Posters need council consent"],
 "Advertising a discount of 25% or more on alcohol where it can be seen or heard from outside the premises is an offence; the discount itself is lawful inside."),
("A pub runs a promotion with a cartoon superhero aimed at teenagers. Which offence may this be?",
 "Promotion appealing to minors", ["Selling outside licensed hours", "Failing to show the licence", "Breaching the manager's register"],
 "Promoting or advertising alcohol in a way that has special appeal to minors is an offence under the Act."),
("A bar puts a sign on the street offering a free shot to everyone who arrives before 9pm. Under the Act this is:",
 "An offence, as it is seen outside", ["Always permitted", "Allowed with a special licence", "Allowed before 9pm"],
 "Promoting or advertising free alcohol in a way that can be seen or heard from outside the licensed premises is an offence, as is offering goods or prizes on condition that alcohol is bought."),
("A licensed venue sells alcohol 40 minutes after its licensed closing time. This is:",
 "An offence by the licensee", ["Allowed for regulars", "Allowed if the door is locked", "A matter for the LAP only"],
 "Selling alcohol outside the hours of the licence is an offence and can also lead to suspension of the licence."),
("What is a controlled purchase operation?",
 "A supervised test buy by a minor", ["A stocktake run by the licensee", "A council audit of drink prices", "A bulk stock delivery to the bar"],
 "In a controlled purchase operation the police send a minor, under supervision, to try to buy alcohol to test whether premises check ID."),
("A licensing inspector arrives on a busy Friday night without notice. What can the inspector do?",
 "Enter and inspect the premises", ["Only enter with a warrant", "Only enter after closing", "Only enter with the owner"],
 "Licensing inspectors and police may enter licensed premises at any reasonable time to check compliance with the Act and the licence."),
("A fight breaks out and spreads through the bar. What power do the police have under the Act?",
 "Order the premises closed", ["Cancel the licence on the spot", "Fine every patron inside", "Seize the duty manager's car"],
 "The police may order licensed premises closed for a period where there is a riot, fighting or serious disorder, a serious threat to public safety, or an emergency."),
("What is a holding recorded against a licensee?",
 "An offence finding on record", ["A licence fee owed", "A share in the business", "A storage area for stock"],
 "When certain offences, such as sales to minors or intoxicated people, are established, a holding is recorded; repeated holdings can lead to licence suspension or cancellation."),
("A 17-year-old buys wine using a driver licence her older sister lent her for that purpose. Which is true?",
 "Both sisters may be fined", ["Only the store can be fined", "No offence if she is served", "Only the younger sister is liable"],
 "Using someone else's ID to buy alcohol is an infringement offence, and so is giving or lending ID to a person you know intends to use it to buy alcohol."),
("A patron shows an ID card that has clearly been altered. What should staff do?",
 "Refuse service and record it", ["Serve one drink only", "Keep the card as a souvenir", "Ask for a verbal promise"],
 "Presenting a false ID is an offence. Staff refuse service, follow the venue's policy on retaining or recording the document, and note the incident."),
("A 17-year-old is found drinking in a bar's restricted area with her mother beside her. What applies?",
 "Minors may not be in that area", ["It is fine with a parent", "It is fine if she is eating", "It is fine before 9pm on weekdays"],
 "No minor may be in a restricted area, even with a parent. A parent's presence matters only in a supervised area."),
("Which offence can staff commit even when the manager is on duty?",
 "Selling alcohol to a minor", ["Failing to write a local alcohol policy", "Renewing the licence late", "Changing the venue layout"],
 "Any person who sells or supplies alcohol to a minor on licensed premises commits an offence, not just the licensee or manager."),
("In many New Zealand towns, drinking in the street at night breaks:",
 "A council alcohol ban", ["The purchase age rule", "The one-way door rule", "The duty manager rule"],
 "Councils make alcohol control bylaws (alcohol bans) for public places; breaking them is an infringement offence. Staff can remind patrons not to take drinks outside."),
("A patron tries to leave a bar carrying an open bottle of beer into a street with an alcohol ban. What should staff do?",
 "Stop the drink leaving", ["Let the patron go", "Pour it into a plastic cup", "Charge a takeaway fee"],
 "Letting open drinks leave the premises undermines the alcohol ban and good order, and many licences have conditions against it."),
("When must staff ask a patron to prove their age?",
 "Whenever they may be under 18", ["Only when they order spirits", "Only after 10pm at weekends", "Only if they order a jug of beer"],
 "Staff must be satisfied the buyer is not a minor. Many venues require ID from anyone who looks under 25 so that no minor slips through."),
("A licensee is convicted of selling alcohol to minors twice within months. Who may apply to ARLA to suspend the licence?",
 "The police or an inspector", ["Any rival licensee", "The Medical Officer of Health only", "The local Member of Parliament"],
 "The police and licensing inspectors can apply to ARLA to suspend or cancel a licence, and ARLA weighs the history of holdings."),
("A server sells a bottle of vodka to a man who is obviously intoxicated. Who may be charged?",
 "The server, manager and licensee", ["Only the intoxicated man himself", "Only the licensee of the bar", "Only the server"],
 "Selling or supplying to an intoxicated person exposes the server, the manager on duty and the licensee to action."),
("A bar owner tells staff a patron is a friend and can be served though he is intoxicated. What should staff do?",
 "Refuse service to the patron", ["Serve him as instructed", "Serve him only beer", "Serve him if he stays seated quietly"],
 "No instruction from an owner overrides the law; serving an intoxicated person is an offence for whoever serves him."),
("A licensee offers a free bar tab to the patron who drinks the most shots. Which offence is this likely to be?",
 "Encouraging excessive drinking", ["Selling outside hours", "Failing to show the licence", "Trading without a local alcohol policy"],
 "Promotions that reward drinking quantity encourage excessive consumption, which is an offence under the Act's promotion rules."),
]
